            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Américo Paredes was a renowned folklorist, ethnographer, educator, novelist, poet, musician, and prolific scholar from Brownsville, Texas. With the support of his wife, Amelia Sidzu Nagamine Paredes, he earned three degrees within a span of six years, becoming the first Mexican-American to receive a doctorate degree from the University of Texas. His dissertation about border corridos and the story of Gregorio Cortez, "
                },
                {
                  "text": "With His Pistol in His Hand, ",
                  "italic": true
                },
                {
                  "text": "became \"a foundational text and primer for the emerging academic movement of Chicano studies\" (Lamadrid, 2018). He was a pioneer in Mexican American studies and Chicano literature, inspiring several generations of Latino scholars at the University of Texas at Austin and beyond. "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "These interviews were recorded for the "
                },
                {
                  "text": "Los del Valle Oral History Project",
                  "bold": true,
                  "link": "https://scholarworks.utrgv.edu/losdelvalle/"
                },
                {
                  "text": ", begun in 1993 by Manuel F. Medrano for the purpose of \"collecting and preserving the historical recollections of people in South Texas researched by scholars and underdocumented in archives and published research\" (Medrano, 2019)."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": ""
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Presented here are ten unedited and original interviews collected in 1994 and 1995 with Dr. Américo Paredes, and one interview with his older brother Lorenzo Paredes. Across these interviews Américo Paredes chronicles his life, academic journey and career as a writer and educator. "
                }
              ]
            },
